      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the area of the region \\( S \\) which is the set of the reciprocals of the complex numbers outside a regular hexagon centered at the origin with opposite pairs of sides one unit apart and one pair of sides parallel to the imaginary axis.\n\nFirst, let's determine the vertices of the hexagon. Since the hexagon has opposite sides one unit apart and one pair of sides parallel to the imaginary axis, the hexagon is symmetric and centered at the origin. The vertices of the hexagon can be expressed as:\n\\[ 1, i, -1, -i, -1 + i, 1 + i \\]\nThese vertices are derived from the fact that the distance between consecutive vertices (each spaced 60 degrees) is \\( \\sqrt{2} \\).\n\nThe region \\( R \\) consists of all complex numbers \\( z \\) such that \\( |z| > 1 \\) but \\( \\text{Im}(z) \\leq 1 \\). The complement of \\( R \\) in the complex plane is the region where \\( \\text{Im}(z) > 1 \\).\n\nThe region \\( S \\) is given by \\( \\left\\lbrace \\frac{1}{z} \\mid z \\in R \\right\\rbrace \\). The set \\( S \\) consists of the reciprocals of the complex numbers in \\( R \\), which means we need to find the region where \\( |z| < 1 \\) but \\( \\text{Im}(z) > 1 \\).\n\nTo find the area of \\( S \\), we can use the fact that the area of \\( S \\) is the area of the semicircle with radius 1 (which is the region where \\( \\text{Im}(z) > 1 \\)) minus the area of the region where \\( |z| < 1 \\), which is a sector of a circle with radius 1 and angle \\( \\pi \\).\n\nThe area of the semicircle with radius 1 is:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{2} \\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nThe area of a sector of a circle with radius 1 and angle \\( \\pi \\) is:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1^2 \\cdot \\pi = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of \\( S \\) is:\n\\[ \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{2} = 0 \\]\n\nHowever, this result is incorrect because the region \\( R \\) includes the upper half of the circle with radius 1, and the reciprocal of a complex number in \\( R \\) will have its image in the lower half of the complex plane. The correct area of \\( S \\) is:\n\\[ \\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nThus, the area of \\( S \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\]",
