      "text": "To find the area of the region \\( S \\), we first need to determine the area of the original hexagon and then use the property of the transformation \\( \\frac{1}{z} \\).\n\nA regular hexagon inscribed in a circle of radius \\( r \\) has side length \\( s = r \\). Since the opposite sides of the hexagon are one unit apart, the radius \\( r \\) of the circumscribed circle is \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\) units. The side length \\( s \\) of the hexagon is equal to the diameter of the circle, so \\( s = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\).\n\nThe area \\( A \\) of a regular hexagon with side length \\( s \\) is given by:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} s^2\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( s = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\):\n\\[\nA = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\left( \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} = 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nSo, the area of the hexagon is \\( 2\\sqrt{3} \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the area of the region \\( S \\), where \\( S = \\left\\{ \\frac{1}{z} \\mid z \\in R \\right\\} \\). To do this, we use the property that the area of the transformation \\( f(z) = \\frac{1}{z} \\) maps the area of \\( R \\) to the reciprocal of the area of \\( R \\). Therefore, the area of \\( S \\) is the reciprocal of the area of the hexagon:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of } S = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of \\( S \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}}\n\\]",
